# ...but the report generators write under report/<topic>/, and the committed
# Cython benchmark is the one artefact that must stay reviewable there. Without
# this negation the bare pattern above matches at ANY depth, so moving the file
# silently untracked it while the README kept linking to it.
!report/benchmarks/cython_optimizer_performance.md
docs/micro_deformable_mirror/docs/shiliang_extracted*
!docs/**/*.png
!report/**/*.png
docs/**/*.npy
report/**/*.npy
# The device-less beam-shaping benchmark was meant to be ignored here, which left
# README pointing at a 1-line smoke leftover because the real 9-cell grid could not
# be committed. The rule was written for `benchmarks/device_less/` while the
# directory is `benchmarks/device_less_full/`, so it never matched anything and the
# whole block was dead -- the artefacts have in fact always been tracked. It is
# ~100 KB and is the authoritative artefact, so: no ignore rule, and an explicit
# negation so a future blanket `report/**` rule cannot silently untrack it.
# (An ignore rule would also be self-defeating here: git does not descend into an
# excluded directory, so the per-file negations below a `dir/**` line would never
# be reached.)
!report/benchmarks/device_less_full/
!report/benchmarks/device_less_full/**
# `*.npz` is ignored above because every other .npz in this repo is a probe/run
# artefact under data/. This one is *source data*: the 66 measured Zernike base
# maps that ZernikeCentroidCalculator needs (R-34). It replaced 66 tracked ASCII
# .txt files (56.4 MB -> 23.2 MB, bit-exact float64). Without the negation a
# fresh clone would raise FileNotFoundError from
# ao_shaping.utils.wavefront.wavefront_calc.get_zernike_base_matrixs.
!scripts/tuning_devices/stdWavefront/std_wavefront.npz
